# Identity bootstrap

{{ .inputs.repo_name }} is its own identity provider (the Substrate `identity` module). There is no open
first-run setup over HTTP: `POST /api/setup` is refused (`setup_offline`). The owner and the first
machine keys are created on the host, before the service starts.

```sh
export SUBSTRATE_CONFIG=/path/to/{{ .inputs.repo_name }}.yaml   # your override file; never committed

# 1. the owner: the password is typed twice at the terminal and not echoed
{{ .inputs.repo_name }} identity setup --username owner --display-name "Owner"

# 2. one key per machine caller, written once to a new 0600 file
{{ .inputs.repo_name }} identity key create --name my-caller --scope {{ .inputs.repo_name }}.read --out /path/to/my-caller.key

# 3. start, then check
{{ .inputs.repo_name }} serve &
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:{{ .inputs.http_port }}/api/setup    # {"setup_over_http":false,"setup_required":false}
```

For automation only, `identity setup` reads the password from the first line of stdin when stdin is not a
terminal; supply it from your secret store, never from a shell variable, the command line or shell history.

The data directory is `${{ .computed.env_prefix }}_DATA_DIR`, else `$XDG_DATA_HOME/{{ .inputs.repo_name }}`, else
`~/.local/share/{{ .inputs.repo_name }}`: the database, the identity signing key (never lose or commit it) and the secrets
state. Details: Substrate `docs/identity.md`.
